  <testcase name="compareResizeTiming" classname="myPractice.round1.datastructures.JavaHashMapBehaviorTest" time="0.012">
    <system-out><![CDATA[=== RESIZE BEHAVIOR COMPARISON ===
Initial capacity: 16, Load factor: 0.75, Threshold: 12

🔹 OUR HASHTABLE BEHAVIOR:
  ⚡ RESIZE at element 13: size 12->13, load factor 0.750->0.406
     → Resize happened BEFORE insertion

🔹 JAVA HASHMAP BEHAVIOR (based on source code analysis):
  Java HashMap uses: if (++size > threshold) resize()
  This means:
  1. Insert the element first (++size)
  2. Then check if size > threshold
  3. If true, resize AFTER insertion

  So Java HashMap would resize at element 13:
  - Insert element 13: size becomes 13
  - Check: 13 > 12? YES → resize
  - Resize happens AFTER insertion

🔹 KEY DIFFERENCE:
  Our implementation: size >= capacity * loadFactor (BEFORE insertion)
  Java HashMap:       ++size > threshold (AFTER insertion)

  Both resize at the 13th element, but at different times:
  - Ours: Resize first, then insert
  - Java: Insert first, then resize
]]></system-out>
  </testcase>
  <testcase name="demonstrateTheoreticalDifference" classname="myPractice.round1.datastructures.JavaHashMapBehaviorTest" time="0.001">
    <system-out><![CDATA[=== THEORETICAL RESIZE TIMING ===

📊 OUR APPROACH (Proactive):
  for (int i = 1; i <= 13; i++) {
    if (size >= capacity * loadFactor) resize(); // Check BEFORE
    insert(element);
  }

  Element 13: size=12, 12 >= 12? YES → resize first, then insert
  Result: Never exceeds load factor threshold

📊 JAVA APPROACH (Reactive):
  for (int i = 1; i <= 13; i++) {
    insert(element);
    if (++size > threshold) resize(); // Check AFTER
  }

  Element 13: insert first (size=13), 13 > 12? YES → resize after
  Result: Temporarily exceeds threshold before resizing

🎯 BOTH ARE VALID APPROACHES:
  ✅ Proactive (Ours): Prevents threshold violation
  ✅ Reactive (Java):  Simpler logic, slightly more efficient
  ✅ Both maintain O(1) average performance
]]></system-out>
